# PIXELS THE ACCEPTANCE WALK'S COLOUR KEY CALLS SKY AND THAT ARE NOT SKY.
#
# `tools/mask-truth-walk.mjs` derives its own sky TRUTH per frame — a hue band
# either side of the covered sky's circular mean, above a saturation floor. It
# has no other condition, and measurement says it cannot get one: a branch
# thrown far out of focus reads SMOOTHER than the sky at one pixel and
# identical to it at eight (NOTES, 2026-09-20), so the field's standard fix of
# colour-plus-texture does not separate them. Brightness does not either — the
# block that provoked this sits 1.8 MADs below typical sky.
#
# So the correction is declared rather than derived. Each row names ONE
# CONNECTED COMPONENT of keyed-but-uncovered pixels, by a point inside it, and
# asserts that component is not sky. A component rather than a rectangle on
# purpose: a box drawn round a corner also swallows the real sky above it, and
# excluding real sky makes the test EASIER, which is the one direction a
# correction must never move.
#
# A ROW IS AN ASSERTION ABOUT A PHOTOGRAPH, NOT AN EXEMPTION. It says somebody
# opened that frame, magnified that place, and saw what is there. The reason is
# required and must say what was seen, not that the check was inconvenient.
#
# FOUR GUARDS, because a file like this is otherwise a way to buy green:
#   - every row is PRINTED on every run, with the size it removed;
#   - a row matching no component FAILS as stale — it cannot rot quietly;
#   - a row with no reason after the em dash FAILS;
#   - a row removing more than 10% of the frame's keyed sky FAILS, whatever its
#     reason. A correction that large is a broken key, not a correction.
#
# Take a row off by fixing the key, never by widening the row.
#
#   <frame>  <x> <y>  — what is actually there, seen

# WITHDRAWN 2026-09-20, and the file is deliberately left empty rather than
# deleted. One row was added here asserting that an 18,933 px band in
# NIR_1651's bottom-right corner is a defocused foreground branch. It took the
# acceptance walk green. The assertion could not be defended when it was
# checked: the band is 94 px wide by 335 tall hard against the frame edge, its
# luminance is flat across the whole of it, and the magnified crop it was
# judged from was taken from x >= 0.80 when the band starts at x = 0.90 — so
# what was described may have been something sitting beside it.
#
# The green went with it. See NOTES.md and decision 023 for what is being done
# to settle what that band actually is.
